      title: "The Synoptic Problem",
      body: `## A literary puzzle

Why are Matthew, Mark, and Luke so alike, and yet so different? This question, known as the **Synoptic problem**, has occupied scholars for more than two centuries. The similarities are too close to be explained by independent oral tradition alone. Consider the story of the healing of the paralytic (Mark 2:1–12; Matthew 9:1–8; Luke 5:17–26). All three include the same awkward break in the middle of a sentence, where Jesus turns from his critics to the paralyzed man: "that ye may know that the Son of man hath power on earth to forgive sins, (he saith to the sick of the palsy,) I say unto thee, Arise." Such a parenthesis, shared word for word, points to literary dependence: one Gospel has copied from another, or all from a common source.

## The data

When the three are compared, several patterns appear.

- Almost all of Mark appears in Matthew, and much of it in Luke.
- Where Matthew and Luke both follow Mark, they usually follow his order; where one departs from Mark's order, the other usually does not.
- Matthew and Luke share about two hundred verses not found in Mark, mostly sayings of Jesus—including the Lord's Prayer and the Beatitudes in their forms—often in very close wording, but placed in quite different contexts.
- Each also has material of its own: Matthew's story of the Magi, Luke's parables of the Good Samaritan and the Prodigal Son.

## The two-source hypothesis

The solution held by most scholars today is the **two-source** (or two-document) **hypothesis**, developed in the nineteenth century. It holds:

1. **Mark was written first** ("Markan priority"), and Matthew and Luke each used Mark independently.
2. Matthew and Luke also used a second source, now lost, consisting mostly of sayings of Jesus. It is called **Q**, from the German *Quelle*, "source."
3. Each also drew on traditions of his own, often labeled "M" for Matthew and "L" for Luke.

The arguments for Markan priority are cumulative. Mark is the shortest Gospel, and it is easier to explain why Matthew and Luke would add material (birth stories, resurrection appearances, sermons) than why Mark would omit it. Mark's Greek is rougher, and Matthew and Luke often seem to smooth it. Mark sometimes says things that might have embarrassed later readers, and Matthew and Luke soften them. In Mark, Jesus "could there do no mighty work" in Nazareth because of unbelief (Mark 6:5); Matthew writes that "he did not many mighty works there" (Matthew 13:58). In Mark a rich man calls Jesus "Good Master," and Jesus replies, "Why callest thou me good?" (Mark 10:18); Matthew rephrases the question (Matthew 19:17).

## The question of Q

Q is a hypothetical document. No manuscript of it survives, and no ancient writer mentions it. Its existence is inferred from the material common to Matthew and Luke but absent from Mark. Scholars who accept it have reconstructed its contents and even proposed stages of composition; others regard such reconstructions as speculative.

A significant minority of scholars accept Markan priority but dispense with Q. The **Farrer hypothesis**, named for the Oxford scholar Austin Farrer and defended more recently by Mark Goodacre in *The Case Against Q* (2002), holds that Luke used both Mark and Matthew, which would explain the shared non-Markan material without a lost source. Another minority view, the **Griesbach** or two-Gospel hypothesis, holds that Matthew was written first, then Luke, and that Mark combined and abbreviated them—a view close to the order in which the Gospels were traditionally placed. Most scholars find it less persuasive, but it reminds us that the Synoptic problem is a hypothesis about evidence, not a settled fact.

## Why it matters

The solution to the Synoptic problem is not merely a technical matter. If Matthew and Luke used Mark, then their changes to Mark reveal their own concerns. This is the basis of **redaction criticism**. When Matthew adds to Peter's confession "Thou art the Christ" the words "the Son of the living God," followed by Jesus's blessing, "thou art Peter, and upon this rock I will build my church" (Matthew 16:16–18; compare Mark 8:29), we learn something about Matthew's interest in Peter and in the church. When Luke changes Jesus's last words on the cross from "My God, my God, why hast thou forsaken me?" (Mark 15:34) to "Father, into thy hands I commend my spirit" (Luke 23:46), we learn something about Luke's portrait of Jesus as the innocent and serene martyr.

The Synoptic problem also bears on the historical Jesus. If Mark and Q are the earliest sources, then traditions found in both—"multiple attestation"—have a strong claim to be early. And if a saying appears only in Matthew's or Luke's special material, historians will ask whether it reflects early tradition or the evangelist's own hand.

> **Pause and consider.** Compare Mark 15:34 with Luke 23:46. Does recognizing that Luke has changed Mark make Luke less trustworthy, or does it help us understand what Luke wanted his readers to see?`,
