      title: "The Kingdom of God and the Parables",
      body: `## "Thy kingdom come"

The phrase "**kingdom of God**" (in Matthew usually "kingdom of heaven," a reverent Jewish way of avoiding the divine name) appears about a hundred times in the Synoptic Gospels and is the center of Jesus's preaching there. It means God's reign—God acting as king—rather than a territory. It draws on the Scriptures' praise of God as king ("The Lord shall reign for ever and ever," Exodus 15:18) and on prophetic hopes that God would one day establish his rule over Israel and the nations.

The prayer Jesus taught his disciples expresses this hope:

> Our Father which art in heaven, Hallowed be thy name. Thy kingdom come. Thy will be done in earth, as it is in heaven.
>
> — Matthew 6:9–10 (KJV)

## Present or future?

Is the kingdom present already, or still to come? The sayings point both ways. On the one hand, Jesus speaks of the kingdom as future and imminent: "There be some of them that stand here, which shall not taste of death, till they have seen the kingdom of God come with power" (Mark 9:1). He promises that many "shall come from the east and west, and shall sit down with Abraham, and Isaac, and Jacob, in the kingdom of heaven" (Matthew 8:11). At the Last Supper he says he will not drink wine again "until that day that I drink it new in the kingdom of God" (Mark 14:25).

On the other hand, the kingdom is somehow already present in Jesus's work: "if I with the finger of God cast out devils, no doubt the kingdom of God is come upon you" (Luke 11:20); "the kingdom of God is within you," or "among you" (Luke 17:21).

Scholars have proposed several resolutions. Schweitzer emphasized the future, apocalyptic kingdom ("consistent eschatology"). The British scholar **C. H. Dodd** argued for "realized eschatology": the kingdom had arrived in Jesus's ministry. Many scholars now speak of an "already and not yet": the kingdom is breaking in through Jesus's healings, exorcisms, and table fellowship, but its full arrival lies in the future.

## Signs of the kingdom

Jesus's actions as well as his words announced the kingdom. Historians widely agree that he was known as a healer and exorcist; even his opponents, according to the Gospels, accused him of casting out demons by the prince of demons (Mark 3:22), and later rabbinic sources call him a sorcerer. Whatever one makes of the miracles, they were understood in the Gospels as signs of the kingdom's power over sickness and evil.

Jesus also ate with "**publicans and sinners**," a practice that scandalized his critics: "This man receiveth sinners, and eateth with them" (Luke 15:2). Meals were powerful symbols of acceptance in the ancient world, and Jesus's table fellowship acted out the kingdom's welcome to the outsider. The **calling of the Twelve**, matching the twelve tribes of Israel, symbolized the restoration of the whole people of God.

## Parables

Jesus's most characteristic form of teaching was the **parable** (Greek *parabolē*, Hebrew *mashal*), a short story or comparison drawn from everyday life. The Gospels contain about forty. Some are brief similes: "The kingdom of heaven is like to a grain of mustard seed … which indeed is the least of all seeds: but when it is grown, it is the greatest among herbs" (Matthew 13:31–32). Others are full narratives: the Good Samaritan, the Prodigal Son, the laborers in the vineyard, the wicked tenants.

For centuries, Christian interpreters read the parables as allegories in which every detail had a hidden meaning. Augustine famously read the Good Samaritan as the story of salvation: the man who fell among thieves is Adam, Jerusalem is the heavenly city, the thieves are the devil and his angels, the Samaritan is Christ, the inn is the church. In 1888–1899 the German scholar **Adolf Jülicher** rejected allegory and argued that each parable makes a single point. Later scholars, notably **Joachim Jeremias** in *The Parables of Jesus*, tried to recover the original setting of each parable in Jesus's ministry. Others have emphasized that the parables are open-ended and provocative: they surprise, reverse expectations, and invite the hearer to decide.

Consider the Good Samaritan (Luke 10:25–37). A lawyer asks, "Who is my neighbour?" Jesus tells of a man robbed and left half dead on the road from Jerusalem to Jericho. A priest and a Levite pass by. The one who stops and helps is a **Samaritan**, a member of a people despised by many Jews as heretics and half-breeds. Jesus then asks a different question: "Which now of these three, thinkest thou, was neighbour unto him that fell among the thieves?" The lawyer cannot bring himself to say "the Samaritan"; he answers, "He that shewed mercy on him." The parable does not answer the question "who is my neighbor?" so much as overturn it.

## Ethics of the kingdom

Jesus's ethical teaching, gathered especially in the Sermon on the Mount and Luke's Sermon on the Plain, is demanding: love of enemies, non-retaliation, generosity without expecting return, prayer in secret, freedom from anxiety about possessions. Interpreters have long debated whether it was meant as a rule for all, an ideal to show human sinfulness, a discipline for a special few, or an "interim ethic" (Schweitzer's term) for the short time before the end. Its power, in any case, has outlasted every interpretation.

> **Pause and consider.** Read the parable of the Prodigal Son (Luke 15:11–32). With which character does the story ask the hearer to identify? Why might Jesus have ended it without telling us whether the elder brother went in?`,
